WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:22.760
My name is Amit and 18 months ago I had another job at Google and I pitched this idea of doing

00:22.760 --> 00:28.640
something with museums and art to my boss who is actually here and she allowed me to

00:28.640 --> 00:35.700
do it. It took 18 months, a lot of fun negotiations and stories I can tell you with 17 very interesting

00:35.700 --> 00:41.120
museums from 9 countries but I'm going to focus on the demo. There are a lot of stories

00:41.120 --> 00:45.960
about why we did this. I think my personal story is explained very simply in the slide

00:45.960 --> 00:53.000
and it's access. I grew up in India, had a great education, not complaining, but I didn't

00:53.000 --> 00:57.880
have access to a lot of these museums and these artworks. When I started traveling and

00:57.920 --> 01:03.040
going to these museums I started learning a lot and while working at Google I tried

01:03.040 --> 01:09.720
to put this desire to make it more accessible with technology together. So we formed a team,

01:09.720 --> 01:14.320
a great team of people and we started doing this. I'm going to probably get into the demo

01:14.320 --> 01:20.160
and then tell you a couple of the interesting things we've had since launch. So simple,

01:20.160 --> 01:25.120
you come to googleartproject.com, you look around all these museums here, you've got

01:25.120 --> 01:29.880
the Uffizi, you've got the MoMA, the Hermitage, the Rikes, the Van Gogh. I'm going to actually

01:29.880 --> 01:35.840
get to one of my favorites, the Metropolitan Museum of Art in New York. Two ways of going

01:35.840 --> 01:41.760
in, very simple, click and bang, you're in this museum, doesn't matter where you are,

01:41.760 --> 01:46.760
Bombay, Mexico, doesn't really matter. You move around, you have fun, you want to navigate

01:46.760 --> 01:53.320
around the museum, open the plan up and in one click jump. You're in there, you want

01:53.320 --> 02:04.600
to go to the end of the corridor, keep going, have fun, explore. Thanks, I haven't come

02:04.600 --> 02:11.720
to the best part. So now I'm in front of one of my favorite paintings, The Harvesters by

02:11.720 --> 02:15.880
Peter Bruegel at the Met. I see this plus sign, if the museum has given us the image,

02:15.880 --> 02:20.120
you click on it. Now this is one of the images, so this is all the metadata information for

02:20.120 --> 02:24.000
those of you who are truly interested in art, you can click this, but I'm going to

02:24.000 --> 02:28.840
click this off right now. This is one of these images that we captured in what we call gigapixel

02:28.840 --> 02:33.840
technology. This image, for example, has close to I think around 10 billion pixels. I get

02:33.840 --> 02:38.160
a lot of people asking me, what do you get for 10 billion pixels? I'm going to try and

02:38.160 --> 02:43.000
show you what you really get for 10 billion pixels. You can zoom around very simply, you

02:43.000 --> 02:48.840
see some fun stuff happening here. I love this guy, his expression is priceless. But

02:48.840 --> 02:53.200
then you really want to go deep. And so I started playing around, and I found something

02:53.200 --> 02:59.440
going on over here. And I was like, hold on, that sounds interesting. Went in, and I started

02:59.440 --> 03:04.280
noticing that these kids were actually beating something. I did a little research, spoke

03:04.280 --> 03:08.560
to a couple of my contacts at the Met, and I actually found out that this is a game called

03:08.560 --> 03:14.640
Skull, which involves beating a goose with a stick on Shrove Tuesday. And apparently

03:14.640 --> 03:18.080
it was quite popular. I don't know why they did it, but you know, I learned something

03:18.080 --> 03:22.720
about it. Now, just, you know, we're really deep in, you can really get the cracks. Now

03:22.720 --> 03:27.640
just to give you some perspective, I'm going to zoom out so you really see what you get.

03:27.640 --> 03:44.240
Here is where we were, and this is the painting. So, the best is yet to come, so in a second.

03:44.320 --> 03:50.520
So now, let's just quickly jump into the MoMA, again in New York. So, another one of my favorites,

03:50.520 --> 03:54.680
the Starry Night. Now, the example I showed you was all about finding details. But what

03:54.680 --> 03:58.320
if you want to see brush strokes? And what if you want to see, you know, how Van Gogh

03:58.320 --> 04:02.680
actually created this masterpiece? You zoom in, you really go in, I'm going to go to one

04:02.680 --> 04:08.200
of my favorite parts in this painting, and I'm really going to get to the cracks. This

04:08.200 --> 04:13.660
is the Starry Night, I think, you know, never seen like this before. I'm going to show you

04:13.660 --> 04:17.680
my other favorite feature. There's a lot of other stuff here, but I don't have time.

04:17.680 --> 04:22.300
This is the real cool part. It's called Collections. Any one of you, anybody, doesn't matter if

04:22.300 --> 04:26.860
you're rich, if you're poor, if you have, you know, a fancy, you know, house, doesn't

04:26.860 --> 04:30.580
matter. You can go and create your own museum online, create your own collection across

04:30.580 --> 04:35.100
all these images. Very simply, you go in, and I've created this called The Power of

04:35.100 --> 04:40.620
Zoom. You can just zoom around. This is the Ambassadors, based in the National Gallery.

04:40.620 --> 04:44.740
You can annotate the stuff, send it to your friends, and really get a conversation going

04:44.740 --> 04:50.700
about what you're feeling when you go through these masterpieces. So I think in conclusion,

04:50.700 --> 04:55.740
for me, the main thing is that all the amazing stuff here does not really come from Google.

04:55.740 --> 04:59.220
It doesn't, in my opinion, even come from the museums. Probably shouldn't say that.

04:59.220 --> 05:04.700
It really comes from these artists. And, you know, that's been my humbling experience in

05:04.700 --> 05:09.820
this. I mean, I hope in this digital medium that we do justice to their artwork and represent

05:09.820 --> 05:15.180
it properly online. And the biggest question I get asked nowadays is, did you do this to

05:15.180 --> 05:20.660
replicate the experience of going to a museum? And the answer is no. It's to supplement the

05:20.660 --> 05:22.780
experience. And that's it. Thank you.

